Analyze this entire React project and extract the coding conventions it follows.
Your goal is to produce a single markdown file (copilot-instructions.md) that another
AI agent can use to write new React code in a different project that follows exactly
the same conventions.

Rules:
- Base everything on what you actually see in the code. Do NOT invent rules or
  add generic React best practices that this project does not follow.
- If a pattern is used consistently, state it as a rule. If it is inconsistent,
  list it under "Unclear / inconsistent" instead of guessing.
- For every rule, include a short real example taken from the project
  (with the file path it came from).
- Write rules as imperative instructions ("Use X", "Never do Y").

Cover at least these areas:
1. Folder and file structure (where components, hooks, services, types, utils,
   pages, assets live; how features are organized)
2. Naming conventions (files, components, hooks, variables, constants, types/interfaces, CSS classes)
3. Component style (function vs class, props typing, default exports vs named exports,
   file layout order, size limits, composition patterns)
4. State management (local state, context, redux/zustand/etc., server state)
5. API calls and data fetching (client setup, error handling, loading states)
6. Routing
7. Styling approach (CSS modules, styled-components, Tailwind, etc.)
8. Forms and validation
9. Error handling and logging
10. TypeScript usage (if applicable): typing rules, interfaces vs types, strictness
11. Testing (framework, file location, naming, what gets tested, mocking approach)
12. Linting, formatting, and tooling config (ESLint, Prettier, tsconfig, etc.)
13. Libraries used and the rule for when to use which
14. Import ordering, path aliases, comments and documentation style
15. Git/commit/PR conventions if they are documented in the repo

Also read README, config files (package.json, eslint, prettier, tsconfig, vite/webpack)
and any existing docs for stated conventions.

Finish with a "Checklist before submitting code" section that the agent can run
against its own output.
